<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05H4">Remark <span data-tag="05H4">10.1.4.18</span>.</a> Definition <a data-tag="05H3" href="/tag/05H3">10.1.4.17</a> has an obvious counterpart for semisimplicial objects. If $u: X_{\bullet } \rightarrow Y_{\bullet }$ is a morphism between semisimplicial objects of an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, we say that $u$ <em>exhibits $Y_{\bullet }$ as an $n$-coskeleton of $X_{\bullet }$</em> if $Y_{\bullet }$ is $n$-coskeletal and the morphism $u$ induces an isomorphism $X_{m} \rightarrow Y_{m}$ for $0 \leq m \leq n$. By virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="05H1" href="/tag/05H1">10.1.4.15</a>, this recovers Definition <a data-tag="05H3" href="/tag/05H3">10.1.4.17</a> in the case where $u$ arises from a morphism between simplicial objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. </p>
